In your API routes, import the `cors` function and use it before defining your route handler. For example:

```javascript
// pages/api/users.js
import cors from '../../pages/cors';
import { getUsers } from '../../services/users';

export default async (req, res) => {
  await cors(req, res);

  if (req.method === 'GET') {
    const users = await getUsers();
    res.status(200).json(users);
  } else {
    res.status(405).json({ error: 'Method not allowed' });
  }
};
```

4. Update your WordPress settings:

In your WordPress dashboard, go to Settings > General and add your Next.js application's URL to the "Allowed Links" field. This will allow WordPress to trust requests from your Next.js application.

