Here's an example of how you can use Google Apps Script to search for a place name in a column and store the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ 'address': placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == google.maps.GeocoderStatus.OK) {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      // Store the latitude and longitude in other columns
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude);
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude);
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue("");
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue("");
    }
  });
}
```

This script will search for the place name in column A and store the latitude and longitude in columns B and C, respectively. Make sure to replace `"A2:A"` with the actual range of cells containing the place names you want to search.

Here's the PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" as requested:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        return $order === 'asc' ? $a['age'] - $b['age'] : $b['age'] - $a['age'];
    });
    return $set;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with your set and order parameters:

```php
