To set up Auth0 SSO (Single Sign-On) for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Create an Auth0 account and set up a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, go to the "Applications" tab and select your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` library using npm or yarn:
```bash
npm install @auth0/auth0-react
```
or
```bash
yarn add @auth0/auth0-react
```
5. In each app, create a new file named `auth0.js` and import the necessary modules:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider } from '@auth0/auth0-react';
```
6. In the `auth0.js` file, create a new instance of the `Auth0Provider` and pass in the `Domain` and `Client ID` values you copied earlier:
```javascript
const auth0Provider = new Auth0Provider(
  'your_domain',
  'your_client_id'
);
```
7. Wrap your entire app component with the `Auth0Provider`:
```javascript
function MyApp() {
  // ...

  return (
    <Auth0Provider>
      {/* Your app content goes here */}
    </Auth0Provider>
  );
}
```
8. In each app, create a new page or component that will handle the authentication flow. For example, you can create a `Login` component like this:
```javascript
import { useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

function Login() {
  const { login } = useAuth0();

  const handleLogin = () => {
    login();
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log in</button>
  );
}
```
9. Finally, you can use the `Login` component in your app to allow users to log in. For example, you can add a button to your app's header that calls the `handleLogin`

Here's a C function that converts an array of pointers (argv) to a single null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *argv_to_str(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i;
    size_t len = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // add space for the null terminator
    }

    char *str = malloc(len);
    if (str == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    i = 0;
    while (i < argc) {
        strncpy(str, argv[i], strlen(argv[i]));
        str += strlen(argv[i]);
        i++;
    }

    *str = '\0';
    return str;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *str = argv_to_str(argc, argv);
    if (str != NULL) {
        printf("Converted string: %s\n", str);
        free(str);
    } else {
        printf("Error converting argv to string.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

