To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, follow these steps:

1. Create a new file named `background.js` in your Chrome extension folder. This file will contain the code for your background script.
2. In `background.js`, add the following code to create a content script that will be injected into the target webpage:
```javascript
chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
  const tabId = tabs[0].id;
  chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabId, {file: 'content.js'});
});
```
This code will query for the active tab and execute the `content.js` script in the background of the tab.
3. Create a new file named `content.js` in your Chrome extension folder. This file will contain the code for your content script.
4. In `content.js`, add the following code to inject the JavaScript code into the target webpage:
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = chrome.runtime.getURL('your_javascript_file.js');
  script.async = true;
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
This code will listen for the `DOMContentLoaded` event and insert a `<script>` tag into the webpage's body. The `src` attribute of the `<script>` tag will point to your JavaScript file, which will be loaded asynchronously.
5. Replace `your_javascript_file.js` with the name of your actual JavaScript file. For example, if your JavaScript file is named `my_script.js`, replace `your_javascript_file.js` with `my_script.js`.
6. Save all the files and refresh your Chrome browser. Your Chrome extension should now inject the JavaScript code into the target webpage.

Note: Make sure to replace `your_javascript_file.js` with the name of your actual JavaScript file, and ensure that the JavaScript file is located in the same folder as your Chrome extension's other files.

