To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can add a simple check for distance between each planet before adding it to the list. If the distance is less than a certain threshold, you can generate a new planet until one with sufficient distance is found. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to pass the desired minimum distance as an argument when calling this function. For example, if you want to ensure a minimum distance of 5 units between planets, you would call the function like this:

